    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Use your ggplot2 (and ggpubr) plots directly with canvasXpress\"\ndescription: \"You don't have to rewrite your plots. Hand an existing ggplot object to canvasXpress() and get an interactive, reproducible version — pairwise significance brackets included.\"\nauthor: \"Isaac Neuhaus\"\ndate: \"2026-09-25\"\ncategories: [R, canvasXpress, ggplot2, ggpubr, visualization]\nimage: \"thumbnail.png\"\n---\n\n\n\nIf you already build plots with **ggplot2** or **ggpubr**, you don't need to learn a new plotting\nAPI to make them interactive. [canvasXpress](https://canvasxpress.org) accepts a **ggplot object\ndirectly**: pass it to `canvasXpress()` and it renders an interactive, reproducible version of the\nsame figure — you keep your existing code.\n\n## Install\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ninstall.packages(c(\"canvasXpress\", \"ggpubr\"))\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n## Wrap a plot you already have\n\nHere's an ordinary `ggpubr` boxplot with **pairwise comparisons** (`geom_pwc`) — the significance\nbrackets that show a test between each pair of groups. Build it exactly as you normally would:\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(ggplot2)\nlibrary(ggpubr)\n\ng <- ggboxplot(iris, x = \"Species\", y = \"Sepal.Width\", fill = \"Species\") +\n  geom_pwc(method = \"t_test\", label = \"p.format\")\n```\n:::\n\n\n\nNow make it interactive — **one call, the same object**:\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(canvasXpress)\n\ncanvasXpress(g)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"htmlwidget-816c9b0e3a797dc49aae\" style=\"width:100%;height:433px;\" class=\"canvasXpress html-widget\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-816c9b0e3a797dc49aae\">{\"x\":{\n  \"renderTo\": \"canvas\",\n  \"data\": [\n    [\"Id\", \"Sepal.Length\", \"Sepal.Width\", \"Petal.Length\", \"Petal.Width\", \"Species\"],\n    [\"1\", \"5.1\", \"3.5\", \"1.4\", \"0.2\", \"setosa\"],\n    [\"2\", \"4.9\", \"3\", \"1.